## Describe the bug

In `addTreeToXML()` (`src/xml_parsing.cpp`), the `name` attribute is only written for
non-subtree nodes. For `SubTreeNode`, only `ID` (and `_fullpath` when metadata is enabled)
is emitted:

```cpp
if(const auto* subtree = dynamic_cast<const SubTreeNode*>(&node))
{
  elem = doc.NewElement(node.registrationName().c_str());
  elem->SetAttribute("ID", subtree->subtreeID().c_str());
  if(add_metadata)
  {
    elem->SetAttribute("_fullpath", subtree->config().path.c_str());
  }
}
else
{
  elem = doc.NewElement(node.registrationName().c_str());
  elem->SetAttribute("name", node.name().c_str());   // <-- only for non-subtree nodes
}
```
As a result, the instance name of a SubTree node is silently lost when a tree is exported
with WriteTreeToXML() and re-imported later.

To Reproduce
1. Create a tree containing a SubTree node instantiated with a custom name, e.g.:
<SubTree ID="MySubTree" name="my_instance"/>
2. Export the tree with WriteTreeToXML(tree, false, false).
3. Observe the output:
<SubTree ID="MySubTree"/>
The name="my_instance" attribute is missing.
4. Re-parse the exported XML: the node's name() now falls back to the default
(registration name), so the original instance name cannot be recovered.

Expected behavior
WriteTreeToXML() should preserve the instance name of every node, including SubTree
nodes, so that export → import round-trips are lossless:
<SubTree ID="MySubTree" name="my_instance"/>
This matters for tooling that serializes a running tree and reloads it later (logging,
editing, tree-diffing), where node names are used as stable identifiers.

Proposed fix
Always set the name attribute, regardless of node type:
elem = doc.NewElement(node.registrationName().c_str());
elem->SetAttribute("name", node.name().c_str());
if(const auto* subtree = dynamic_cast<const SubTreeNode*>(&node))
{
  elem->SetAttribute("ID", subtree->subtreeID().c_str());
  if(add_metadata)
  {
    elem->SetAttribute("_fullpath", subtree->config().path.c_str());
  }
}

Environment
- Branch/version: master (observed on 4.10.0+)
- OS / Compiler: all (pure serialization logic)

BehaviorTree.CPP uses `nonstd::expected` (vendored in `include/behaviortree_cpp/contrib/expected.hpp`)
with `std::string` as the error type, e.g.:

- `Blackboard::getStamped<T>()` → `Expected<Timestamp>`
- `TreeNode::getInput<T>()` → `Expected<T>`
- `JsonExporter::fromJson<T>()` → `Expected<T>`
- `Any::toJsonString()` → `Expected<std::string>`

When user code accesses `.value()` on an unexpected result, a `bad_expected_access<std::string>`
is thrown. However, its `what()` implementation ignores the stored error and returns a fixed
literal:

```cpp
virtual char const * what() const noexcept override
{
    return "bad_expected_access";
}
```

To Reproduce
auto bb = BT::Blackboard::create();
try
{
  // key does not exist / type mismatch -> unexpected(std::string) with a detailed message
  auto res = bb->getStamped<int>("missing_key").value();
}
catch(const std::exception& ex)
{
  std::cout << ex.what() << std::endl;
}
Actual output:
bad_expected_access
The real diagnostic (e.g. Blackboard::get() missing key 'missing_key') is swallowed.
Expected behavior
what() should surface the underlying error message, since in BT.CPP the error type is
almost always std::string and already contains a human-readable description:
Blackboard::get() missing key 'missing_key'
Without this, any exception-based error handling (or crash logs showing only what())
loses the actual failure reason, making debugging significantly harder.

Proposed fix
Precompute the message in the constructor (keeping what() noexcept), falling back to
the original literal for error types that are not convertible to std::string:
```cpp
explicit bad_expected_access( error_type error )
: m_error( error )
, m_what( make_what(error) )
{}

virtual char const * what() const noexcept override
{
    return m_what.c_str();
}

private:
static std::string make_what( error_type const & e )
{
  if constexpr( std::is_convertible_v<error_type, std::string> )
  {
    return std::string( e );
  }
  else
  {
    return "bad_expected_access";
  }
}

error_type m_error;
std::string m_what;
```
Note: the bad_expected_access<void> specialization (used by expected<T, void>) is
unaffected.

Environment
- Branch/version: master (observed on 4.10.0+)
- OS / Compiler: all (header-only vendored expected.hpp)
